6868057251]]}, {"t": " public", "lp": -0.9203939437866211, "top": [[" public", -0.9203939437866211], [" user", -1.0498191118240356]]}, {"t": " mode", "lp": -0.24759742617607117, "top": [[" mode", -0.24759742617607117], [" CCS", -2.257418394088745]]}, {"t": "**", "lp": -0.0009460750734433532, "top": [["**", -0.0009460750734433532], ["**.", -6.985158920288086]]}, {"t": " in", "lp": -0.48075661063194275, "top": [[" in", -0.48075661063194275], [" for", -0.999284565448761]]}], "text": "Based on current technical documentation and driver behavior for Intel Arc GPUs (including the A770) under the Linux `xe` driver (or the newer `intel-mesa`/`i915` drivers), here is the breakdown:\n\n### 1. **CCS Mode 1 vs. Mode 2**\n\n**CCS (Constant Color Surface)** modes refer to how the GPU handles **color space conversions** and **gamma correction** during rendering, particularly when using **YUV** or **HDR** content.\n\n- **CCS Mode 1**:  \n  - **Standard sRGB / Linear workflow**.  \n  - Used for **SDR (Standard Dynamic Range)** content.  \n  - Applies standard gamma correction (typically 2.2 or sRGB curve).  \n  - **Default mode** for most desktop compositing and video playback.  \n  - **Use when**: You are displaying normal SDR content (e.g., YouTube videos, desktop UI, games in SDR).\n\n- **CCS Mode 2**:  \n  - **HDR (High Dynamic Range) workflow**.  \n  - Enables **extended color gamut** and **higher luminance** handling.  \n  - Requires **HDR-capable display** and **HDR content** (e.g., HDR10, HLG).  \n  - **Use when**: You are playing HDR video content or using HDR-enabled games on an HDR-capable monitor.\n\n> **Note**: The `xe` driver (and newer `intel-mesa`) may **auto-select** the appropriate CCS mode based on the display\u2019s HDR capabilities and the content type. Manual override is rarely needed unless you are debugging or forcing specific behavior.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **When to Use CCS Mode 4?**\n\n**CCS Mode 4** is **not a standard public mode** in"}